From ce011a94246b786dae707f61680f9a26a46f554c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 10:09:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Master tune from mastertune: A4 at 415.3 to 466.2 Hz for
 voices, audio clips, CV and MIDI; recordings marked

Ports mastertune 1.2.1's master tune (its patch 0001 and the hook 0005 moved) onto v1.3. model/tuning/master_tune*
are mastertune's files with the flash migration taken out; the hooks are fitted to v1.3.

- Settings > Tuning > Master tune (Hz): the reference pitch of A4, 415.3 to 466.2 Hz in steps of 0.1 Hz. Kept with
  the community settings as "masterTune" in tenths of Hz (mastertune's name and unit). A value out of range in the
  file is ignored (440 Hz), not clamped: "440" (Hz, not tenths) gave 415.3 Hz in mastertune.
- Everything that makes sound follows it: synth and kit voices, every oscillator type (sample, wavetable, the basic
  waves, FM carriers and modulators, DX7), audio clips (pitch-shifted, their length kept), the CV outputs, and external
  MIDI by RPN 1 (Channel Fine Tuning): before a note on a channel that hasn't got the current tuning, to every channel
  the song plays on when the tuning changes and when playback starts (a task of its own), never more than 18 bytes
  into a DIN buffer that has more than 256 waiting. Also v1.3's own MIDI output of a synth or kit row (its MIDI menu).
  Held notes retune at once (a voice's generation checked at each render; the CV pitch outputs re-sent).
- Audio recorded on the Deluge at another tuning gets an "mtun" chunk (4 bytes, the tuning in tenths of Hz) before
  its data, so it is shifted only by the difference later and never tuned twice; other software skips the chunk. Live
  input used as an oscillator already sounds at the current tuning and is left alone.
- At 440.0 Hz everything not recorded at another tuning plays as without it (the ratio is exactly one).
- Two faults found by the review, v1.3's own: Settings > Actions > Reset settings (community features, or all) left
  the tuning, and the CPU monitor's mode (features/0002), as they were, and leaving the menu wrote them straight
  back: both now go back to their defaults. A CV instrument whose CV2 carries an expression (v1.3: aftertouch, mod or
  velocity) would have had that voltage replaced by an old pitch at a tuning change: only a channel whose last
  voltage was a pitch is re-sent.
- Left out: the migration from the flash bytes 198-199 of early master-tune builds (the next free bytes in v1.3's
  flash layout; the tuning is in the community settings file since mastertune v16).
